WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.313 --> 00:00:01.980
SPEAKER 1: The ability
for your business

00:00:01.980 --> 00:00:04.740
to tackle the
unpredictable is critical,

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:07.320
whether tackling unexpected
shifts in supply chains

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:09.360
or surges in customer demand.

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:11.070
At the core, you
need a database that

00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:13.950
can back your business,
power next gen applications,

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:16.740
and leaves all of the
management to somebody else

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:18.060
without breaking the bank.

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:20.800
So you don't have to worry
about quick shifts any longer.

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:23.280
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:28.120
We demand a lot
of our databases,

00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:30.430
and our databases
demand a lot of us.

00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:34.180
Your database can be a
powerful engine or a management

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:35.200
headache.

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:38.230
Traditionally, databases tend
to be fragile, expensive,

00:00:38.230 --> 00:00:39.880
and tough to maintain.

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:41.770
Without one that can
scale without limits,

00:00:41.770 --> 00:00:45.250
provide data consistency, and
eliminate management overhead,

00:00:45.250 --> 00:00:48.340
you're faced with an inability
to scale with new business

00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:51.970
needs, data inconsistencies that
can risk losing your customers'

00:00:51.970 --> 00:00:56.110
trust, and cost inefficiencies,
since IT teams are tied up

00:00:56.110 --> 00:00:58.960
maintaining those systems
instead of innovating.

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:00.850
Your developers
wind up implementing

00:01:00.850 --> 00:01:03.100
their own complicated
database sharding--

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:05.319
or partitioning--
strategy, which

00:01:05.319 --> 00:01:08.200
leads to overprovisioning
of resources and ballooning

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:11.260
costs, not to mention the
cost of managing backups,

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:13.510
scheduling maintenance
windows, meeting

00:01:13.510 --> 00:01:17.170
performance requirements, and
securing the entire thing.

00:01:17.170 --> 00:01:20.620
You end up having to pick
between scale, consistency,

00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:22.330
or management-free.

00:01:22.330 --> 00:01:24.400
You can't have it all--

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:26.740
until we created
Google Cloud Spanner,

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:29.500
a fully-managed relational
database service

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:32.710
that scales horizontally,
is strongly consistent,

00:01:32.710 --> 00:01:34.420
and speaks SQL.

00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:37.720
Spanner offers an
industry-leading 99.999%

00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:40.270
availability with
automatic software updates

00:01:40.270 --> 00:01:41.880
and instant optimizations.

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:45.160
You get zero
downtime-- yes, zero--

00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:46.810
and no maintenance windows.

00:01:46.810 --> 00:01:49.480
It's used to power
workloads large and small,

00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:52.840
from applications for
just a few to massive apps

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:54.460
with global users.

00:01:54.460 --> 00:01:56.680
Spanner is simple
to use and manage,

00:01:56.680 --> 00:01:58.330
so you can build
applications that

00:01:58.330 --> 00:02:00.730
transform your
business while lowering

00:02:00.730 --> 00:02:02.530
your Total Cost of Ownership--

00:02:02.530 --> 00:02:03.730
TCO.

00:02:03.730 --> 00:02:05.270
Let me show you how.

00:02:05.270 --> 00:02:07.660
First off, Spanner offers scale.

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:10.150
For relational databases,
significant effort

00:02:10.150 --> 00:02:12.730
is required to grow a
cluster horizontally

00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:16.540
to support additional capacity,
and setting up data replication

00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:19.480
in different parts of the
world has never been easy.

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:22.510
You're left with an extremely
complex architecture requiring

00:02:22.510 --> 00:02:25.000
a specialized team to maintain.

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:28.450
With Spanner, you don't need
to shard or replicate data

00:02:28.450 --> 00:02:31.300
nor build intricate frameworks
to scale up quickly.

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:34.960
It vastly simplifies your
architecture and operations

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:37.330
because it automatically
distributes data

00:02:37.330 --> 00:02:39.940
and automatically handles
replicas, sharding,

00:02:39.940 --> 00:02:44.260
and transaction processing
across regions and continents

00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:47.080
so you can quickly scale
to meet any usage pattern,

00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:49.660
deliver data closest
to your end users

00:02:49.660 --> 00:02:51.640
with the lowest
possible latency,

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:53.890
and ensure success
of your products.

00:02:53.890 --> 00:02:56.050
With a single click
or API call, you

00:02:56.050 --> 00:02:59.620
can start with small workloads
and scale with no limits.

00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:01.360
Choosing Spanner
means you won't need

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:04.690
to rewrite your applications
or migrate databases,

00:03:04.690 --> 00:03:07.630
even as future
data volume grows.

00:03:07.630 --> 00:03:09.400
This brings me to
my next point--

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:10.960
consistency.

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:13.240
Traditional
databases force users

00:03:13.240 --> 00:03:16.360
to choose between
performance and consistency.

00:03:16.360 --> 00:03:19.690
With eventual consistency,
you risk serving stale data

00:03:19.690 --> 00:03:22.390
to customers, which
might not be acceptable

00:03:22.390 --> 00:03:25.660
in time-sensitive situations
like retail, finance,

00:03:25.660 --> 00:03:26.920
and gaming.

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:29.140
For example, showing
an item in stock

00:03:29.140 --> 00:03:32.380
online when in reality,
it may be zero.

00:03:32.380 --> 00:03:36.820
This can lead to a bad customer
experience and loss of trust.

00:03:36.820 --> 00:03:40.480
Spanner uses True Time, which
leverages a fully redundant

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:42.580
system of atomic clocks.

00:03:42.580 --> 00:03:44.560
That means Spanner
applies a timestamp

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:47.020
based on a globally
agreed-upon clock

00:03:47.020 --> 00:03:49.660
to every transaction on commit.

00:03:49.660 --> 00:03:51.220
Transactions in
different regions

00:03:51.220 --> 00:03:54.850
are executed sequentially--
no ifs, ands, or buts

00:03:54.850 --> 00:03:56.740
on the commit timestamp.

00:03:56.740 --> 00:03:59.140
And finally, high availability--

00:03:59.140 --> 00:04:03.340
Spanner offers up to an
industry-leading 99.999%

00:04:03.340 --> 00:04:06.760
availability, so you're
protected from failures.

00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:07.740
How?

00:04:07.740 --> 00:04:09.660
Spanner provide
high availability

00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:12.400
via a synchronous
replication between replicas

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:14.050
in independent zones.

00:04:14.050 --> 00:04:17.410
It uses a Paxos base replication
scheme in which writes

00:04:17.410 --> 00:04:21.310
are committed only when a
majority quorum is achieved.

00:04:21.310 --> 00:04:24.250
You can choose between regional
and multi-regional replicas

00:04:24.250 --> 00:04:26.800
that span continents
across the globe.

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:28.630
Unlike typical
database services,

00:04:28.630 --> 00:04:30.940
Spanner does not need
any maintenance windows

00:04:30.940 --> 00:04:32.170
or downtime.

00:04:32.170 --> 00:04:34.960
Spanner are also offers
online schema changes,

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:37.960
meaning easy schema
updates, no downtime,

00:04:37.960 --> 00:04:40.730
no disruption for your users.

00:04:40.730 --> 00:04:43.330
Spanner is
enterprise-proven, secure,

00:04:43.330 --> 00:04:45.690
and battle-tested at Google.

00:04:45.690 --> 00:04:48.370
With features like manage
backup and restore and open

00:04:48.370 --> 00:04:51.520
source emulator for local
testing, a variety of client

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:54.370
libraries and
performance tuning tools,

00:04:54.370 --> 00:04:57.160
you can achieve business
continuity, lower development

00:04:57.160 --> 00:04:59.350
costs, and empower
your developers

00:04:59.350 --> 00:05:01.660
to get started right away.

00:05:01.660 --> 00:05:04.750
Meanwhile, data-layer
encryption, IAM integration

00:05:04.750 --> 00:05:08.110
for access and controls, and
comprehensive audit logging

00:05:08.110 --> 00:05:11.070
give you enterprise-grade
security.

00:05:11.070 --> 00:05:13.450
Spanner's been used in
industries like gaming

00:05:13.450 --> 00:05:16.030
for user profiles and
real-time play data,

00:05:16.030 --> 00:05:18.680
fintech for account
data and ledgers,

00:05:18.680 --> 00:05:21.310
healthcare for medical
records and patient data,

00:05:21.310 --> 00:05:24.390
retail for supply chain
and inventory management,

00:05:24.390 --> 00:05:27.720
tech for real-time decision
making and performance data,

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:30.720
and media for digital rights
management and catalog

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:31.860
metadata.

00:05:31.860 --> 00:05:34.590
Spanner is the enterprise
database giving you

00:05:34.590 --> 00:05:37.620
the best of relational
and nonrelational features

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:41.790
with transactional consistency,
SQL support, automatic sharding

00:05:41.790 --> 00:05:45.930
and replication, and
nearly unlimited scale.

00:05:45.930 --> 00:05:49.290
Cost-effectively streamline
your IT operations,

00:05:49.290 --> 00:05:52.080
give your end users the
experience they demand,

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:55.380
give yourself peace of mind
through its reliability,

00:05:55.380 --> 00:05:57.600
and build transformative
applications

00:05:57.600 --> 00:05:59.370
at the speed of your business.

00:05:59.370 --> 00:06:03.510
Try it out for yourself by going
to cloud.google.com/spanner.

00:06:03.510 --> 00:06:05.640
And stay tuned to hear
more about Spanner.

00:06:05.640 --> 00:06:08.090
[MUSIC PLAYING]

